.TH tubase64 n 0.2 tclutils
.SH tclutils::tubase64
.PP
Base64 helpers using Tcl core \fBbinary encode base64\fR and \fBbinary decode base64\fR.
.SS API
.CS
set b64 [::tclutils::tubase64::encode $bytes]
set data [::tclutils::tubase64::decode $b64]
.CE
.PP
Commands:
.IP \(bu
\fBencode data ?\-maxlen n? ?\-encoding binary|utf\-8?\fR
.IP \(bu
\fBdecode text ?\-encoding binary|utf\-8?\fR
.IP \(bu
\fBencodeFile path ?\-maxlen n?\fR
.IP \(bu
\fBdecodeFile path\fR
.SS "Bytes and text"
.PP
\fBencode\fR takes bytes by default (\fB\-encoding binary\fR): each character is one byte. A character above 255 is \fB{TCLUTILS TUBASE64 BYTES}\fR on Tcl 8.6 and 9.0 alike; Tcl 8.6 alone would keep only the low byte. For text pass \fB\-encoding utf\-8\fR; \fBdecode \-encoding utf\-8\fR turns the bytes back into text (0.2).
.CS
::tclutils::tubase64::encode "Jörg €" -encoding utf-8   ;# SsO2cmcg4oKs
::tclutils::tubase64::decode SsO2cmcg4oKs -encoding utf-8 ;# Jörg €
::tclutils::tubase64::encode "Jörg"                      ;# SvZyZw== (ö as byte f6)
.CE
.PP
\fBtuhash\fR and \fBtuxxhash\fR default to \fButf\-8\fR instead, because they are mostly used on text; \fBtubase64\fR, \fBtubase32\fR and \fBtucrc\fR default to bytes, because their input is mostly file or image data.
.PP
Errors: \fBMAXLEN\fR, \fBBYTES\fR, \fBENCODING\fR.
